WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:02.960
What a character chooses to drink
says a lot about them.

00:00:03.440 --> 00:00:05.200
- A martini, shaken, not stirred.

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:07.280
If they get a shot of whiskey, they're cool.

00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:10.520
If they order a fine chardonnay,
they're probably trying to go for class.

00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:13.760
But there's one drink used in cinema
that requires a bit of explanation.

00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:17.500
- I see you're drinking one percent,
is that 'cause you think you're fat?

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:22.420
Probably the first thing that comes to mind
when you think of milk is youth and innocence.

00:00:22.420 --> 00:00:25.980
When a sixteen year old uses
his charm and grace to mimic being a pilot,

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:28.820
we're reminded of his age by his drink choice.

00:00:28.820 --> 00:00:30.280
- Would you like a drink after take-off?

00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:31.480
- Milk?

00:00:31.940 --> 00:00:34.040
In 'A Clockwork Orange', the adolescents drink milk,

00:00:34.120 --> 00:00:37.560
a gentle reminder that all this mayhem
is being caused by kids.

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:41.620
James Dean famously nurses a bottle of milk
in such a way that we get a visual cue

00:00:41.620 --> 00:00:44.180
of his conflict between maturity and adulthood.

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:46.200
Of course, if you don't drink milk then you're manly.

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:49.020
- How tough am I? How tough am I?!

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:51.380
I had a bowl of nails for breakfast this morning...

00:00:51.380 --> 00:00:52.379
- He, yeah. So?

00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:55.080
- ...without any milk.

00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:57.140
- Uh, right this way. Sorry to keep you waiting.

00:00:57.140 --> 00:01:00.420
If you want to go for a deep reading,
you could say that Kevin spilling milk in 'Home Alone'

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:03.120
is a symbol of his lack of maternal nourishment.

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:07.320
Or you could say John Hughes just wanted the visual joke of everybody crying over spilled milk.

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:09.220
But this only scratches the surface.

00:01:09.220 --> 00:01:12.580
Many adults also drink milk,
and that's where it gets more complicated.

00:01:12.580 --> 00:01:18.040
Milk is a symbol of childishness so it's humorous to see 'Leon the Professional', a hyper masculine assassin,

00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:19.180
drinking it throughout the movie.

00:01:19.180 --> 00:01:23.480
It fits into the comedic moments of the movie,
but it also sheds light on his maternal side

00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:25.300
as he takes Matilda under his wing.

00:01:25.300 --> 00:01:29.640
In this instance drinking milk is a joke,
but many times drinking milk has the opposite intent:

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:30.480
It's creepy.

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:32.440
There's something off about a grown man drinking milk,

00:01:32.440 --> 00:01:35.240
and filmmakers seem to be well aware of this intuition.

00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:38.800
In Hitchcock's 'Suspicion',
the audience suspects that the milk may be poisoned.

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:42.780
It's an eerie choice to have milk,
a drink we associate with innocence and purity,

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:44.260
be the drink that may be poisoned.

00:01:44.260 --> 00:01:48.380
I don't think it would have had nearly the same effect
if it were a cup of coffee or a shot of whiskey.

00:01:48.380 --> 00:01:50.860
There's something delightful about
making milk threatening.

00:01:50.860 --> 00:01:52.580
We see this again in "Inglourious Basterds".

00:01:52.580 --> 00:01:55.540
Colonel Hans Landa chugs a glass of milk
from LaPadite's farm,

00:01:55.540 --> 00:01:58.340
and it has the creepy factor
but there's more to it than that.

00:01:58.340 --> 00:02:01.580
I quote the social theorist Roland Barthes
and his take on milk:

00:02:19.780 --> 00:02:22.000
So when a villain grabs a glass of milk it's creepy,

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:23.340
but it also shows their power.

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:27.540
They're conquering the pure and innocent
by consuming it, and there's a strength to that.

00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:31.960
When I saw "Mad Max: Fury Road" in theaters,
the only time people audibly cringed in the crowd

00:02:31.960 --> 00:02:35.040
wasn't during an action sequence,
it was when this happened.

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:42.180
Western culture has an aversion to breast milk,
seeing it as taboo to drink as an adult.

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:44.000
- Tastes a little *sniff* funky.

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:46.560
- That's because that's from Debby's left breast, Greg.

00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:51.980
The choice to harvest breast milk in the movie further alienates the post-apocalyptic world from our own.

00:02:51.980 --> 00:02:54.720
In short 'Mad Max: Fury Road' is a movie about fluids.

00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:57.980
Women are exploited for their breast milk,
men are exploited for their blood,

00:02:57.980 --> 00:03:01.240
and the populace is exploited
by controlling the water supply.

00:03:01.240 --> 00:03:05.220
The complete control of these liquids by Immortan Joe shows his dominion over his world,

00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:06.840
and the milk is no exception.

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:09.440
In fact, his sons drink the milk
to keep up their muscle mass,

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:12.500
reinforcing the idea that consuming milk
is a sign of strength.

00:03:12.500 --> 00:03:14.480
What's so great about using these liquids in the film

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:18.480
is that it allows Mad Max to introduce
plot points and character arcs non-verbally.

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:23.140
After Mad Max performs his first selfless act for Furiosa he washes himself with milk,

00:03:23.140 --> 00:03:27.540
symbolizing the start of his transformation
from a selfish man only looking for survival,

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:29.640
to one who cares and nurtures for others.

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:33.740
The turn to completely trusting Furiosa
is symbolized by him giving her his blood,

00:03:33.740 --> 00:03:35.000
another liquid symbol.

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:39.440
'Mad Max: Fury Road' is a prime example of milk enhancing the visual storytelling of a movie.

00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:41.480
Milk is a powerful storytelling device.

00:03:41.480 --> 00:03:44.260
It can show someone as a child,
but it can also be used to unsettle,

00:03:44.260 --> 00:03:46.880
show character strength,
or give information about the plot.

00:03:46.880 --> 00:03:49.720
So the next time you see a character drinking something, ask yourself:

00:03:52.740 --> 00:03:53.660
Thanks for watching.

00:03:53.660 --> 00:03:57.260
*Summertime by DJ Jazzy Jeff and The Fresh Prince plays* Here it is, a groove, slightly trans-form, just a bit of a break

00:03:57.260 --> 00:04:00.260
from the norm, just a little something to break the

00:04:00.260 --> 00:04:06.640
monotony of all that hard...

